/* Section 32.1 clinical case cards: single best answer. Source: Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry, 12th ed., section 32.1 (Infant, Child, and Adolescent Development). */
KS.add("ch32-1", "cases", [
  {"id":"c321-pl01","tag":"Prenatal life","q":"A 24-year-old woman at 12 weeks of pregnancy asks when she will feel her baby move. Which answer fits the usual timing?","choices":["At 16 to 20 weeks","At 32 to 34 weeks","At 26 to 28 weeks","At 8 to 10 weeks","At 10 to 12 weeks"],"answer":0,"why":"Women usually detect movement at 16 to 20 weeks, though stimulation can produce whole-body motion by 14 weeks (s321-pre)."},
  {"id":"c321-pl02","tag":"Prenatal life","q":"A medical student asks which of the fetal reflexes this section dates (grasp, Moro and sucking) appears first in utero. Which is correct?","choices":["Sucking reflex","Babinski reflex","Rooting reflex","Grasp reflex","Moro reflex"],"answer":3,"why":"The grasp reflex appears at 17 weeks, before the Moro (25 weeks) and sucking (about 28 weeks) reflexes (s321-pre)."},
  {"id":"c321-pl03","tag":"Prenatal life","q":"An infant born at 29 weeks has a hypoxic-ischemic episode. A resident asks which brain tissue was most at risk at that age. Which is the best answer?","choices":["Adult-pattern cerebellar cortex","Developing white matter","Peripheral nerves","Mature association cortex","Spinal motor neurons"],"answer":1,"why":"Developing white matter before 32 weeks is especially sensitive to hypoxic, ischemic and metabolic injury (s321-pre)."},
  {"id":"c321-pl04","tag":"Prenatal life","q":"A neuroscience tutor explains that some neurons make growth factors and then die on schedule. What is this programmed death called?","choices":["Neurulation","Allostasis","Excitotoxicity","Apoptosis","Myelination"],"answer":3,"why":"Neurons that produce neurotrophic factors and die once done undergo apoptosis, part of pruning (s321-pre)."},
  {"id":"c321-pl05","tag":"Prenatal life","q":"A 7-year-old boy with early, severe neglect develops a depressive disorder at age 14, while his sister with the same history does not. A geneticist cites a gene that sensitizes neglected children to later depression. Which gene is it?","choices":["Huntingtin gene","Dopamine D2 receptor gene","Serotonin transporter gene","Apolipoprotein E gene","Monoamine oxidase B gene"],"answer":2,"why":"The chapter's example of the transactional model is the serotonin transporter gene, which raises depression risk after early abuse or neglect (s321-pre)."},
  {"id":"c321-pm01","tag":"Maternal factors","q":"A 30-year-old woman had marked, persistent anxiety through her pregnancy. Her newborn is underweight, irritable and sleeps poorly. Which mechanism best explains this link?","choices":["Maternal antibodies crossing the placenta","Fetal hypothyroidism from maternal anxiety","Stress hormones such as norepinephrine and ACTH reaching the fetus","Lower fetal temperature from maternal stress","Placental infection from anxiety"],"answer":2,"why":"Maternal stress raises epinephrine, norepinephrine and ACTH in the fetal blood, raising fetal blood pressure, heart rate and activity; anxious mothers more often have irritable, low-birth-weight infants (s321-mstress)."},
  {"id":"c321-pm02","tag":"Maternal factors","q":"A 37-year-old pregnant woman with no family history asks whether she needs prenatal testing. What is the best answer?","choices":["No, testing is only for women under 25","Only if a prior child had a defect","Yes, because she is older than 35","Only if ultrasound is abnormal at term","No, testing is only for first pregnancies"],"answer":2,"why":"Prenatal testing is advised for women older than 35 or with a family history of congenital disability (s321-mstress)."},
  {"id":"c321-pm03","tag":"Maternal factors","q":"A 26-year-old woman's prenatal screen shows a high α-fetoprotein level. Which abnormality does this most suggest?","choices":["Tay-Sachs disease","A lipoidosis","A neural tube defect","An X-linked disorder","Trisomy 21"],"answer":2,"why":"High α-fetoprotein levels detect neural tube defects (s321-mstress)."},
  {"id":"c321-pm04","tag":"Maternal factors","q":"A 34-year-old woman is choosing between fetoscopy and amniocentesis and asks which carries the higher miscarriage risk. Which is correct?","choices":["Both under 1 percent","Amniocentesis, about 10 percent","Both about 2 percent","Amniocentesis, about 5 percent","Fetoscopy, about 5 percent"],"answer":4,"why":"About 5 percent of women having fetoscopy miscarry; amniocentesis causes damage or miscarriage in under 1 percent (s321-mstress)."},
  {"id":"c321-pm05","tag":"Maternal factors","q":"A couple learns their newborn has a congenital disability. Weeks later the mother is tearful and says she keeps thinking about the healthy baby she had pictured. What best explains her low mood?","choices":["Stranger anxiety","Maternal fever in pregnancy","Postpartum mania","Loss of the fantasized perfect child","Excitotoxic brain injury"],"answer":3,"why":"Parents may react with guilt, anxiety or anger, and grieving the imagined perfect child can lead to depression (s321-mstress)."},
  {"id":"c321-pd01","tag":"Prenatal exposures","q":"A 2-year-old girl adopted from foster care has short stature, a head circumference below the 3rd percentile, short palpebral fissures, a smooth philtrum and a thin upper lip. She is hyperactive. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Trisomy 21 alone","Tay-Sachs disease","Neonatal narcotic withdrawal","Fetal alcohol syndrome","Congenital toxoplasmosis"],"answer":3,"why":"Prenatal-onset growth retardation, microcephaly, short palpebral fissures, smooth philtrum, thin upper lip and hyperactivity are listed features of fetal alcohol syndrome (Table 32-1)."},
  {"id":"c321-pd02","tag":"Prenatal exposures","q":"A 3-day-old boy is jittery, cries a great deal and vomits severely. His mother used marijuana heavily throughout pregnancy and he was born early and small. Which exposure best explains the picture?","choices":["Lithium","Maternal fever","Tobacco smoking only","Chronic marijuana use","Warfarin"],"answer":3,"why":"Chronic marijuana use is linked to low birth weight, prematurity and withdrawal-like crying, tremors and hyperemesis (s321-mdrug)."},
  {"id":"c321-pd03","tag":"Prenatal exposures","q":"A 2-month-old infant is irritable, cries often and seems to avoid being held or looked at. His mother used crack cocaine in pregnancy. Which effect does this most closely match?","choices":["Behavioral effects of prenatal cocaine","Fetal alcohol syndrome","Neural tube defect","Mechanical deformation","Congenital rubella"],"answer":0,"why":"Prenatal crack cocaine may cause irritability, more crying and less desire for human contact (s321-mdrug)."},
  {"id":"c321-pd04","tag":"Prenatal exposures","q":"A 28-year-old woman with epilepsy plans pregnancy. Which of her possible medications is listed as a common teratogen?","choices":["Iron sulfate","Folic acid","Vitamin D","Acetaminophen","Valproate"],"answer":4,"why":"Valproate, carbamazepine and phenytoin are listed teratogenic anticonvulsants (s321-mdrug)."},
  {"id":"c321-pd05","tag":"Prenatal exposures","q":"A pediatric resident reviews Table 32-2 and asks which cause accounts for the largest share of malformations seen in the first year among those given a percentage. Which is correct?","choices":["Maternal infections","Autosomal genetic disease","Mechanical deformations","Chemicals, drugs and radiation","Preconception exposures"],"answer":1,"why":"Autosomal genetic disease accounts for 15 to 20 percent, more than chromosomal (5), maternal conditions (4), infections (3), mechanical (1 to 2) or chemicals (under 1) (Table 32-2)."},
  {"id":"c321-pd06","tag":"Prenatal exposures","q":"A 22-year-old woman smoked a pack a day throughout pregnancy. Which outcome is most directly linked to smoking in the chapter?","choices":["Neural tube defect","Low birth weight","Tay-Sachs disease","Trisomy 21","Microphthalmia"],"answer":1,"why":"Smoking in pregnancy may cause premature birth and low birth weight; some reports link it to SIDS (s321-mdrug)."},
  {"id":"c321-ir01","tag":"Infant milestones","q":"A 10-month-old girl is brought for a routine visit. She creeps, sits unaided with steady balance, pulls up to stand, points with her index finger, and cries when her mother leaves the room. What is the best interpretation?","choices":["Early sign of an anxiety disorder","Advanced development typical of 2 years","Insecure attachment needing referral","Delayed gross motor development","Normal development for about 40 weeks"],"answer":4,"why":"Table 32-3 lists sitting alone, creeping, pulling to stand, index-finger pointing and separation anxiety at 40 weeks (s321-inf)."},
  {"id":"c321-ir02","tag":"Infant milestones","q":"An 8-month-old boy has a persistent Moro response when his head is allowed to fall back. His grasp reflex is also still strong. What is the best interpretation?","choices":["Normal variant of the tonic neck reflex","Expected, since the Moro reflex lasts until walking","Normal postmature pattern","Expected, since these reflexes last until 12 months","Persistence beyond the expected age that warrants evaluation"],"answer":4,"why":"In healthy children the grasp, Moro and tonic neck reflexes disappear by the fourth month; only the Babinski usually lasts to about 12 months (s321-inf)."},
  {"id":"c321-ir03","tag":"Infant milestones","q":"A 9-month-old has an upgoing toe response with fanning when the sole is stroked. Other reflexes and milestones are normal. What is the best interpretation?","choices":["Retained Moro reflex","Expected finding at this age","Sign of prematurity","Sign of spinal injury","Abnormal finding requiring urgent imaging"],"answer":1,"why":"Babinski usually persists until about month 12, so finding it at 9 months is expected (s321-inf)."},
  {"id":"c321-ir04","tag":"Infant milestones","q":"A baby is born 15 days after the expected date. On exam the baby has long nails, little lanugo, thick scalp hair and is very alert. What is the most likely classification?","choices":["Postmature infant","Term infant with fetal alcohol effects","Low-birth-weight infant","Premature infant","Small fetus for gestational age"],"answer":0,"why":"A baby delivered at least 2 weeks after the due date is postmature; typical signs are extra scalp hair, long nails, sparse lanugo and heightened alertness (s321-inf)."},
  {"id":"c321-ir05","tag":"Infant milestones","q":"A boy was born at 33 weeks weighing 2,300 g. His parents ask which later problem he is at higher risk for. Which answer is supported?","choices":["Persistent Babinski reflex into school age","Early puberty","Dyslexia and other learning disabilities","Accelerated language development","Postmaturity syndrome"],"answer":2,"why":"Birth at 33 weeks and under 2,500 g is preterm by any definition (the WHO cut-off is before 37 completed weeks; the book prints 34). Prematurity raises the risk of dyslexia and other learning disabilities, as well as abuse, intellectual disability and emotional or behavioral problems (s321-inf)."},
  {"id":"c321-ir06","tag":"Infant milestones","q":"A 2-year-old girl at daycare plays with blocks next to another child without interacting, says \"no\" to her mother, and calls herself by her name. What is the best interpretation?","choices":["Oppositional behavior needing treatment","Social delay suggesting autism","Advanced development typical of 5 years","Delay, since joint play is expected by 2 years","Normal development for 2 years"],"answer":4,"why":"Parallel play, saying \"no\" to mother and referring to self by name are all 2-year landmarks in Table 32-3; associative play comes at 4 years (s321-inf)."},
  {"id":"c321-ir07","tag":"Infant milestones","q":"A 4-year-old boy can stand on one foot for 6 seconds, walks down stairs one step per tread, brushes his teeth and plays cooperatively with other children. Which skill would be expected next, at about 5 years?","choices":["Printing his full name","Copying a square","Riding a two-wheel bicycle","Copying a triangle","Tying shoelaces"],"answer":1,"why":"Copying a square is a 5-year landmark; the bicycle, shoelaces, triangle and printing his name are 6-year landmarks in Table 32-3 (s321-inf)."},
  {"id":"c321-ir08","tag":"Infant milestones","q":"A mother of a 4-month-old says her baby now smiles at anyone who talks to her, holds her head steady, and lifts it 90 degrees when lying on her forearms. What is the best interpretation?","choices":["Normal development for 16 weeks","Delayed social smile","Indiscriminate attachment disorder","Advanced development typical of 40 weeks","Delayed head control"],"answer":0,"why":"Table 32-3 lists the spontaneous social (exogenous) smile, balanced head and 90-degree head lift at 16 weeks (s321-inf)."},
  {"id":"c321-il01","tag":"Language","q":"An 18-month-old boy says about 15 words, talks to his toys in long jargon strings, and his speech is about one quarter intelligible. He understands many object names. What is the best interpretation?","choices":["Hearing loss","Echolalia suggesting a developmental disorder","Normal language for 12 to 18 months","Advanced language typical of 3 years","Expressive language delay"],"answer":2,"why":"Table 32-4 lists up to 20 words by 18 mo, jargon mixed with words, and about 25% intelligibility for the single-word stage (s321-lang)."},
  {"id":"c321-il02","tag":"Language","q":"A 2.5-year-old girl uses short sentences with \"the\" and \"can\", tells her mother what she is about to do, and still repeats phrases she hears. Her speech is about two thirds intelligible. What is the best interpretation?","choices":["Advanced true communication stage","Normal grammar formation stage","Receptive language disorder","Echolalia indicating autism spectrum disorder","Expressive delay"],"answer":1,"why":"In the 24 to 36 mo grammar formation stage, children use function words, announce intentions, have echolalia that gradually fades, and are 50% to 80% intelligible (s321-lang)."},
  {"id":"c321-il03","tag":"Language","q":"A 10-month-old turns and vocalizes to his name, copies the tune of speech, shakes his head for \"no\" and says \"oh-oh\". He has no real words. What is the best interpretation?","choices":["Selective mutism","Hearing impairment","Advanced two-word stage","Normal attending-to-language stage","Speech delay; the first word is expected by 6 months"],"answer":3,"why":"Table 32-4 lists these behaviors at 7 to 11 mo; the mean age of the first word is 11 mo (s321-lang)."},
  {"id":"c321-il04","tag":"Language","q":"A 4-year-old girl tells stories about yesterday, uses plurals and past tense, makes rhymes, and is about 90% intelligible with occasional sound-order errors. Which sounds are still expected to be settling after this stage?","choices":["f, v, s, z, l, r and th","t, d, k and g","p, b and m","All vowels","n, w and ng"],"answer":0,"why":"From 55 mo on, s, z, f, v, l, r, th and consonant clusters stabilize; p, b, m and k, g, n, ng, w, h, t, d are already correct earlier (s321-lang)."},
  {"id":"c321-ip01","tag":"Piaget","q":"A 4-year-old boy refuses to sleep because he is afraid the moon is watching him. His parents ask if this is abnormal. Which Piagetian concept best explains it?","choices":["Horizontal decalage","Tertiary circular reaction","Animism in the preoperational period","Transitive inference in the concrete period","Personal fable in the formal period"],"answer":2,"why":"Animistic thinking (fear of the moon) is a preoperational feature at 2 to 7 years in Table 32-5 (s321-piaget)."},
  {"id":"c321-ip02","tag":"Piaget","q":"A 15-month-old repeatedly drops toys from her high chair and watches what happens, exploring how they bounce and roll. Which Piagetian substage is this?","choices":["Secondary circular","Tertiary circular","Preoperational","Reflexive substage","Mental representation"],"answer":1,"why":"Table 32-5 lists tertiary circular reactions, exploring properties and dropping objects, at 12 to 18 mo (s321-piaget)."},
  {"id":"c321-ip03","tag":"Piaget","q":"A 9-year-old boy understands that frozen milk is the same amount once it melts and enjoys sorting his toy cars by type. Which Piagetian period is he in?","choices":["Concrete operational","Preoperational","Sensorimotor","Formal operational","Gestational"],"answer":0,"why":"Conservation of matter and hierarchical classification are concrete operational achievements at 7 to 11 years (s321-piaget)."},
  {"id":"c321-ip04","tag":"Piaget","q":"A 14-year-old girl refuses to go to school with a small pimple, convinced every classmate will stare at her. Which concept best describes this thinking?","choices":["Animism","Centration","Imaginary audience","Transductive reasoning","Irreversibility"],"answer":2,"why":"Imaginary audience, the belief that everyone is looking at them, is listed under formal operations from 11 years onward (s321-piaget)."},
  {"id":"c321-ip05","tag":"Piaget","q":"A 3-year-old says, \"We must take this road since Daddy always takes it.\" Which type of reasoning does this show?","choices":["Transitive inference","Transductive","Propositional","Hypothetical-deductive","Reversibility"],"answer":1,"why":"Transductive reasoning, linking events because they occur together, is listed for 2 to 5 years in Table 32-5 (s321-piaget)."},
  {"id":"c321-ip06","tag":"Piaget","q":"A 20-month-old uses a block as a pretend phone, remembers where his ball was hidden, knows animal sounds and names body parts. Which substage best fits?","choices":["Reflexive (birth to 1 mo)","Secondary circular","Tertiary circular","Concrete operational","Mental representation (18 mo to 2 yr)"],"answer":4,"why":"Pretend play, mental representation, recall of hidden objects, animal sounds and body parts are listed at 18 mo to 2 yr (s321-piaget)."},
  {"id":"c321-ie01","tag":"Emotion and temperament","q":"A mother brings her 5-week-old to a well-baby visit and says he smiles in his sleep and at random moments, but not when she smiles at him. Exam and growth are normal. Which statement best describes this smiling?","choices":["Exogenous smiling, which should already be well established","Early sign of absent social reciprocity needing referral","Stranger anxiety expressed as an atypical smile","Rapprochement behavior seen in Mahler's scheme","Endogenous smiling, expected in the first 2 months"],"answer":4,"why":"Endogenous smiling is spontaneous and unrelated to outside stimulation in the first 2 months; exogenous social smiling to the mother appears by 16 weeks (Emotional and social development)."},
  {"id":"c321-ie02","tag":"Emotion and temperament","q":"A father asks at a 2-month visit when his daughter will smile in reply to his face. She is healthy and currently smiles only spontaneously. By about what age should he expect smiling in response to a caregiver?","choices":["By 40 weeks","By 3 weeks","By 26 weeks","By 8 weeks","By 16 weeks"],"answer":4,"why":"Exogenous smiling, stimulated by a caregiver such as the mother, occurs by the 16th week (Emotional and social development; Table 32-3)."},
  {"id":"c321-ie03","tag":"Emotion and temperament","q":"A 9-month-old was cared for by his mother until she was hospitalized for 4 months. Since then he has been withdrawn and sad despite good physical care by relatives. Which long-term concern does the developmental literature on this period raise?","choices":["Arrest in the normal autism subphase","Depression that can last into adult life and become part of character","Failure to develop the nine temperament dimensions","Inevitable insecure/avoidant attachment to all caregivers","Permanent loss of the endogenous smile"],"answer":1,"why":"A long separation from the mother or main caregiver in the second half of the first year can bring on depression that can last into adult life and become part of character."},
  {"id":"c321-ie04","tag":"Emotion and temperament","q":"A 10-month-old is hungry and fussing, yet breaks into a smile when her father makes a funny face. Her mother asks whether this is unusual. Which explanation fits?","choices":["This is endogenous smiling, which is unrelated to stimulation","Infant mood at this age depends only on internal states","It shows a difficult temperament with low threshold of responsiveness","It means she is in Mahler's symbiosis subphase","In the latter part of the first year, mood is increasingly driven by social cues"],"answer":4,"why":"In the later two-thirds of year one, mood becomes linked to external social cues, so a parent can make even a hungry infant smile."},
  {"id":"c321-ie05","tag":"Emotion and temperament","q":"A researcher rates a 2-year-old on how strong a sound must be before the child reacts. Which Chess and Thomas temperament dimension is being measured?","choices":["Adaptability","Rhythmicity","Threshold of responsiveness","Intensity","Distractibility"],"answer":2,"why":"Threshold of responsiveness is the intensity of a stimulus required for a response; intensity refers to energy level of the response (Table 32-7)."},
  {"id":"c321-ie06","tag":"Emotion and temperament","q":"A nurse rates a 4-year-old on how regular his sleep, hunger and bowel habits are. Which temperament dimension is this?","choices":["Quality of mood","Activity level","Approach/withdrawal","Rhythmicity","Attention span"],"answer":3,"why":"Rhythmicity refers to regulation of bodily functions (Table 32-7)."},
  {"id":"c321-ie07","tag":"Emotion and temperament","q":"A 5-year-old cries when her teacher corrects her drawing and comforts a classmate who fell. Her parents worry she is too sensitive. According to Table 32-6, what is the best interpretation?","choices":["Abnormal: sensitivity to criticism should not appear before 11 years","A sign of a difficult temperament requiring treatment","Expected: by age 5 children show sensitivity to criticism and care about others' feelings","Expected only if the superego already dominates at this age","Abnormal: empathy normally appears only in adolescence"],"answer":2,"why":"Table 32-6 lists sensitivity to criticism and caring about others' feelings by age 5; the superego is said to dominate later, at 7 to 11 years."},
  {"id":"c321-ia01","tag":"Attachment","q":"In a lecture, a student describes infant monkeys who fed from a wire bottle-holding figure but spent most of their time clinging to a cloth-covered figure. What conclusion did this work support?","choices":["Attachment depends mainly on infant temperament","Infant attachment is not simply a product of feeding","Monkeys raised on cloth surrogates mated and mothered normally","Attachment is formed mainly through feeding","Wire surrogates gave better comfort when infants were frightened"],"answer":1,"why":"Harlow's monkeys preferred the terry-cloth surrogate for contact comfort; the results suggest attachment is not simply a product of feeding. Both groups later failed to adjust and mother."},
  {"id":"c321-ia02","tag":"Attachment","q":"A first-time mother fears she will spoil her 3-month-old by picking him up whenever he cries. Based on Ainsworth's findings, what is the best advice?","choices":["Respond only at scheduled feeding times","Let him cry so he learns to self-soothe and becomes independent","Hold him only when he is quiet to reward calm behavior","Hand him to a different caregiver each time to widen attachments","Responding sensitively leads to less crying later and fosters self-reliance"],"answer":4,"why":"Ainsworth found cuddling a crying baby leads to less crying in later months, and close contact on signal fosters self-reliance; unresponsive mothers produce anxious babies."},
  {"id":"c321-ia03","tag":"Attachment","q":"A 15-month-old in a playroom wanders off to explore the toys, glancing back at his mother and returning to her briefly before exploring again. Which concept best explains his ability to explore?","choices":["Normal autism","Ainsworth's secured base effect","Stranger anxiety","Harlow's social isolation effect","Primary maternal preoccupation"],"answer":1,"why":"Ainsworth confirmed attachment reduces anxiety; the secured base effect lets a child leave the attachment figure to explore."},
  {"id":"c321-ia04","tag":"Attachment","q":"A 2-year-old will not go to daycare without her worn blanket, which she carries everywhere and holds when unsure. What term did Winnicott use for this blanket?","choices":["Secure attachment figure","Holding environment","Good-enough object","Transitional object","Object constancy"],"answer":3,"why":"Winnicott called comfort items like a blanket or a teddy bear transitional objects; they also act as a secure base."},
  {"id":"c321-ia05","tag":"Attachment","q":"A 7-month-old in an orphanage with one caregiver per 15 infants and frequent staff changes is markedly delayed despite good nutrition and no infections. She is placed with an adequate foster family. What is the expected course?","choices":["Development of stranger anxiety as the only change","Marked acceleration in development","No change, because early delays are fixed","Improvement only in physical growth, not development","Further decline because of the change in caregivers"],"answer":1,"why":"Spitz and others showed institutional infants with few staff per child and high turnover lag markedly, and the same infants show marked acceleration in adequate foster or adoptive care."},
  {"id":"c321-ia06","tag":"Attachment","q":"An 8-month-old cries and clings when a visiting aunt approaches, even while sitting in her mother's lap. She has been cared for only by her mother. What is the best interpretation?","choices":["Expected stranger anxiety, more likely with a single caregiver","Delayed development, since stranger anxiety should fade by 26 weeks","A difficult temperament in the 10% group","Insecure/ambivalent attachment needing referral","Separation anxiety, which requires separation from the mother"],"answer":0,"why":"Stranger anxiety first appears about 26 weeks, is fuller by 32 weeks, occurs even in the mother's arms, and is more likely with only one caregiver."},
  {"id":"c321-ia07","tag":"Attachment","q":"A mother of a 3-year-old has just given birth to a second child. The 3-year-old's attachment to her seems reduced. Which factor most influences how large this drop is?","choices":["Whether the older child has a transitional object","The newborn's birth weight","How secure, confident and mentally well the mother is","The father's play style","Whether the newborn is a boy or a girl"],"answer":2,"why":"A second child decreases firstborn attachment, most in a firstborn aged 2 to 5 years, and the extent depends on how secure, confident and mentally well the mother is."},
  {"id":"c321-ia08","tag":"Attachment","q":"A 12-month-old is briefly separated from both parents at a clinic. When both return, he crawls straight to his mother. When his mother later steps out, he goes to his father for comfort. Which statement fits?","choices":["It shows the child is in the normal autism subphase","Typical: infants usually choose the mother but turn to the father if she is unavailable","Abnormal: infants should not attach to fathers before age 2","It shows insecure/avoidant attachment to the father","Abnormal: he should prefer the parent who plays with him more"],"answer":1,"why":"Babies attach to fathers as well as mothers; when both parents return after a separation, babies usually pick the mother, and they seek the father when she is not there."},
  {"id":"c321-ia09","tag":"Attachment","q":"A researcher compares two insecurely attached 1-year-olds, one avoidant and one ambivalent, who have similarly sensitive mothers. Which factor is said to determine which kind of insecurity develops?","choices":["Infant temperament","Birth order","The number of caregivers in the home","Maternal sensitivity alone","The father's attachment style"],"answer":0,"why":"Whether attachment is secure depends chiefly on how sensitive and responsive the mother is, but when attachment is insecure the type is determined by infant temperament."},
  {"id":"c321-im01","tag":"Separation-individuation","q":"A 20-month-old girl runs across the park, then rushes back to cling to her mother before running off again. She protests both when held and when left. In Mahler's scheme, which subphase is she in?","choices":["Object constancy","Rapprochement","Practicing","Differentiation","Symbiosis"],"answer":1,"why":"Rapprochement (18 to 24 months): as the child senses helplessness and dependence, the child swings between wanting independence and wanting closeness; the child moves away and returns for reassurance (Table 32-9)."},
  {"id":"c321-im02","tag":"Separation-individuation","q":"A 4-year-old boy plays calmly at preschool all morning and tells his teacher his mother is at work and will pick him up. In Mahler's scheme, which achievement does this reflect?","choices":["Differentiation","Symbiosis","Practicing","Object constancy","Normal autism"],"answer":3,"why":"Object constancy (2 to 5 years): the child is reassured by the permanence of the mother and important others even when they are not present (Table 32-9)."},
  {"id":"c321-im03","tag":"Separation-individuation","q":"A 7-month-old has become more alert, studies faces closely and pulls at his mother's hair and glasses as if examining her as separate from himself. Which Mahler subphase fits best?","choices":["Rapprochement","Differentiation","Symbiosis","Practicing","Object constancy"],"answer":1,"why":"Differentiation (5 to 10 months): neurologic growth and alertness turn attention outward and the infant gradually appreciates being distinct from the mother (Table 32-9)."},
  {"id":"c321-im04","tag":"Separation-individuation","q":"A 13-month-old who has just begun walking spends the visit exploring every corner of the room with delight. In Mahler's scheme, which subphase is this?","choices":["Object constancy","Differentiation","Symbiosis","Rapprochement","Practicing"],"answer":4,"why":"Practicing (10 to 18 months): the ability to move autonomously increases exploration of the outer world (Table 32-9)."},
  {"id":"c321-im05","tag":"Separation-individuation","q":"A tearful mother of a 4-month-old says she must be a bad parent: her baby cries at every noise, sleeps poorly, feeds at random times and cannot be soothed. Her older child was easy. What is the best initial step?","choices":["Recommend placing the baby with a different caregiver","Recognize the infant's difficult temperament and address the mother's feelings of inadequacy","Reassure her that this temperament never affects later development","Diagnose insecure/avoidant attachment","Advise her to respond less so the baby learns to settle"],"answer":1,"why":"Difficult children (10%) are hyperalert, sleep poorly, eat unpredictably and are hard to comfort; they must be recognized because parents often feel inadequate and blame themselves, and most have later emotional disturbances (Parental fit)."},
  {"id":"c321-im06","tag":"Separation-individuation","q":"A study enrolls 200 healthy newborns and classifies their temperament using Chess and Thomas's types. About how many would be expected to be easy children?","choices":["100","20","80","40","160"],"answer":2,"why":"Easy children are 40 percent of children (80 of 200); difficult children 10 percent; the other 50 percent are mixtures (Parental fit)."},
  {"id":"c321-im07","tag":"Separation-individuation","q":"A pregnant woman in her third trimester says she thinks about her baby all day and fantasizes constantly about him. She asks whether this is normal. Which Winnicott concept describes this?","choices":["Goodness of fit","Nonintegration","Primary maternal preoccupation","Rapprochement","Holding environment"],"answer":2,"why":"Winnicott described primary maternal preoccupation, from the last trimester through the first few months, when the mother is absorbed in her baby, in fantasy and in real experience."},
  {"id":"c321-tt01","tag":"Toddler","q":"A 23-month-old girl answers \"no\" to nearly every request from her parents, though she follows routines and plays happily. Her development is otherwise on track. Which is the best interpretation?","choices":["A sign of hearing loss","Early sign of an autism spectrum condition","Oppositional disorder that needs treatment now","Evidence of insecure attachment","Normal negativism that supports developing independence"],"answer":4,"why":"Toddlers typically learn \"no\" before \"yes\"; negativism is vital to independence and suggests an oppositional problem only if it persists (s321-todd)."},
  {"id":"c321-tt02","tag":"Toddler","q":"At a 2-year visit, parents report that their son says about 10 single words but has never put two words together. He follows simple commands. Which is the best next step per most child experts?","choices":["Refer for psychoanalytic play therapy","Reassure and review again at age 4","Order brain imaging","Arrange a hearing test","Start a stimulant trial"],"answer":3,"why":"For a child with no two-word sentences at age 2, most experts advise checking hearing (s321-todd)."},
  {"id":"c321-tt03","tag":"Toddler","q":"A 20-month-old boy sees a large dog at the park, pauses, and looks at his mother's face before deciding whether to approach. Which developmental process does this show?","choices":["Band-Aid phase","Social referencing","Immanent justice","Originality","Parallel play"],"answer":1,"why":"Checking a parent's face for cues before reacting to something new is social referencing, common in year two (s321-todd)."},
  {"id":"c321-tt04","tag":"Toddler","q":"An 18-month-old girl puts her doll to bed, pretends to give it a bottle, and wipes its mouth. Which toddler capacity does this best illustrate?","choices":["Associative play","Immanent justice","Cooperative play","Social referencing","Symbolic activity"],"answer":4,"why":"Toddlers engage in symbolic activities such as playing with dolls that represent something, like a feeding sequence (s321-todd)."},
  {"id":"c321-tt05","tag":"Toddler","q":"Parents of a 3-year-old ask whether it is abnormal that he is dry all day but still wets the bed most nights. Based on usual timelines in the toddler discussion, which is the best response?","choices":["Nighttime control is usually complete by about 4 years, so this is still expected","Night wetting at 3 indicates a neurologic disorder","Nighttime control is usually complete by 2½ years, so he needs workup","Night wetting at 3 suggests harsh toilet training","Daytime and nighttime control usually arrive together"],"answer":0,"why":"Daytime control is usually complete by 2½ and nighttime control by 4 years (s321-todd)."},
  {"id":"c321-tt06","tag":"Toddler","q":"During an assessment, a mother describes punishing her 2-year-old harshly for toilet accidents. Which other feature of her parenting is most likely?","choices":["Encouraging the child to learn from mistakes","Punitive and restrictive practices in other areas","Indifference to the child's behavior","Excessive praise for independence","Highly permissive limit setting"],"answer":1,"why":"Toilet training is a paradigm of family training; a parent overly severe here is likely punitive and restrictive elsewhere (s321-todd)."},
  {"id":"c321-tt07","tag":"Toddler","q":"Parents of a 2-year-old girl worry because she takes about half an hour to fall asleep and asks to be comforted first. She sleeps about 12 hours a day with a 2-hour nap. What is the best response?","choices":["Screen for separation anxiety disorder","Explain that this is typical and offer bedtime reassurance","Refer for a sleep study","Stop the daytime nap","Start a sleep medication"],"answer":1,"why":"A typical 2-year-old needs around half an hour to drop off, sleeps roughly 12 hours a day with a 2-hour nap, and may want comfort at bedtime (s321-todd)."},
  {"id":"c321-tt08","tag":"Toddler","q":"A 2-year-old boy wakes frightened at night, saying the room is \"too dark.\" He is otherwise well. Which simple measure is suggested?","choices":["Moving bedtime later by 3 hours","Removing the afternoon nap","Sending him to daycare","Strict ignoring of cries","A nightlight"],"answer":4,"why":"Toddler sleep difficulties from fear of the dark may be helped with a nightlight (s321-todd)."},
  {"id":"c321-tt09","tag":"Toddler","q":"Parents of an active 2-year-old say they now forbid him from trying anything on his own so he cannot fail. Which guidance best fits the toddler-stage parental task?","choices":["Remove all limits so he can explore freely","Respond only to his physical needs","Continue full control until school age","Use punishment for each mistake","Set firm limits but let him act for himself and learn from mistakes, helping when tasks exceed him"],"answer":4,"why":"Toddler parenting requires firm boundaries plus encouragement of progressive emancipation, avoiding being too authoritarian, with protection when challenges exceed the child (s321-todd)."},
  {"id":"c321-tp01","tag":"Preschool","q":"A 4-year-old girl hides her doll when a friend comes over, then cries when the friend cannot guess where it is, unable to see that her friend never saw it hidden. Which feature of preschool thinking does this show?","choices":["Formal logic","Immanent justice","Egocentric thinking","Social referencing","Concrete operations"],"answer":2,"why":"Preschool thinking is egocentric; children cannot place themselves in another child's position (s321-presch)."},
  {"id":"c321-tp02","tag":"Preschool","q":"A 5-year-old boy insists that every scratch be examined and bandaged by his mother, and he worries about getting hurt. He is otherwise thriving. What is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Sign of physical abuse","Insecure attachment","Normal Band-Aid phase","Obsessive-compulsive disorder","Illness anxiety disorder"],"answer":2,"why":"From 3 to 6 years, worry about sickness and injury is common (the Band-Aid phase), and the child wants a parent to check and treat each one (s321-presch)."},
  {"id":"c321-tp03","tag":"Preschool","q":"A 5-year-old girl says her brother must be punished forever because he broke a rule once, and she cannot accept that he had a reason. Which concept explains her view?","choices":["Originality","Parallel play","Immanent justice","Social referencing","Gender role"],"answer":2,"why":"Until about 7 years, rules are experienced as absolute, and a violation calls for absolute retribution: immanent justice (s321-presch)."},
  {"id":"c321-tp04","tag":"Preschool","q":"At daycare, a 2½-year-old boy builds blocks right beside another child, but the two never speak or share. What type of play is this?","choices":["Rough and tumble play","Parallel play","Dramatic play","Cooperative play","Associative play"],"answer":1,"why":"At 2½ to 3 years, children typically play alone side by side with no interaction: parallel play (s321-presch)."},
  {"id":"c321-tp05","tag":"Preschool","q":"Two 4-year-old girls build a tower together, take turns adding pieces, and negotiate who goes next. According to the preschool discussion, which play type is this?","choices":["Parallel play","Rough and tumble play","Associative play","Cooperative play","Solitary play"],"answer":3,"why":"By age 4, children can share and play cooperatively; real interaction and turn-taking become possible (s321-presch)."},
  {"id":"c321-tp06","tag":"Preschool","q":"A 3-year-old boy draws a person as a circle with eyes, nose and mouth, without arms, legs or torso. Which is the best interpretation?","choices":["Typical early stage of human-figure drawing","Sign of intellectual disability","Abstract, affect-laden adolescent style","Evidence of body-image disturbance","Use of perspective typical of middle childhood"],"answer":0,"why":"A child's first human drawing is a circle with marks for mouth, nose and eyes; other parts are added later and the torso last (s321-presch)."},
  {"id":"c321-tp07","tag":"Preschool","q":"Parents of a bright 4-year-old girl report she talks to a friendly invisible playmate who \"keeps her company\" when she is alone. She has friends and no other concerns. What is the best next step?","choices":["Start an antipsychotic","Refer urgently for psychosis evaluation","Forbid her from mentioning the playmate","Order an EEG","Reassure parents that this is common and usually benign"],"answer":4,"why":"Imaginary companions are common in preschoolers, often of above-average intelligence; they tend to be friendly, ease loneliness and calm anxiety, and up to 50 percent of 3- to 10-year-olds may have one (s321-presch)."},
  {"id":"c321-tp08","tag":"Preschool","q":"Parents ask by what age imaginary companions usually go away. Which answer fits the chapter?","choices":["By age 5","They never resolve","By age 7","By age 3","By age 12, though occasionally later"],"answer":4,"why":"Most imaginary companions are gone by age 12, though a few last into adulthood (s321-presch)."},
  {"id":"c321-tp09","tag":"Preschool","q":"A 4-year-old boy becomes aggressive toward his gifted older sister, whom relatives praise constantly. Which factor most likely aggravates his behavior?","choices":["Band-Aid phase","Immanent justice","Social referencing","Parallel play","Favoritism"],"answer":4,"why":"Favoritism for any reason commonly aggravates sibling rivalry; children given special treatment because they are gifted draw siblings' anger (s321-presch)."},
  {"id":"c321-tp10","tag":"Preschool","q":"Parents of a 3-year-old enroll her in intense daily reading and math drills far beyond her level. She becomes irritable and refuses to go. Which statement best fits the chapter?","choices":["Preschool education has no value","Earlier drills always speed development","Pushing academics well beyond capability can be counterproductive","Her refusal shows oppositional disorder","She needs evaluation for a learning disorder"],"answer":2,"why":"Early schooling can help, yet pushing academics well past a child's abilities tends to backfire (s321-presch)."},
  {"id":"c321-tp11","tag":"Preschool","q":"A 5-year-old boy becomes tearful and clingy whenever he is tired or hungry, though he is otherwise cheerful. What is the best interpretation?","choices":["Sign of neglect","Typical influence of somatic states on preschool emotions","Early mood disorder","Oppositional behavior","Separation anxiety disorder"],"answer":1,"why":"Bodily states like fatigue and hunger still sway preschool feelings easily (s321-presch)."},
  {"id":"c321-ms01","tag":"School age","q":"A 9-year-old girl can now work on homework for long stretches, organizes her thoughts logically and has become strict about the rules of games with friends. Her parents ask whether this is typical. Which developmental period best explains these changes?","choices":["Middle years","Late adolescence","Toddler period","Early adolescence","Preschool period"],"answer":0,"why":"Logical, organized thinking, interest in rules and well-established concentration by 9 or 10 mark the middle years, from age 6 to puberty (Middle years: language and cognitive development)."},
  {"id":"c321-ms02","tag":"School age","q":"An 8-year-old boy's teacher reports that he and two classmates were found comparing genitals in the bathroom. He is otherwise doing well and laughs at toilet jokes like his peers. What is the most accurate interpretation?","choices":["Typical sexual curiosity of the middle years","Early sign of a paraphilic disorder","Sign of sexual abuse that requires immediate removal from home","Evidence of fixation at the oral stage","Symptom of an emerging psychotic disorder"],"answer":0,"why":"Although once called latency, the middle years include ongoing sexual interest; sex play, genital comparison and toilet humor are typical, especially in boys (Middle years: emotional and social development)."},
  {"id":"c321-ms03","tag":"School age","q":"A 7-year-old boy cries and clings each morning and refuses to enter school. His mother, herself very anxious, admits she dreads being apart from him. He also avoids birthday parties and play dates. What is the most likely driver of his school refusal?","choices":["Bullying at school","Sleep deprivation from parasomnias","Conduct problems","Separation anxiety","Specific learning disorder"],"answer":3,"why":"School refusal at this age is usually due to separation anxiety, which a fearful mother may transmit; it is rarely isolated, and these children avoid other social situations too (School refusal)."},
  {"id":"c321-ms04","tag":"School age","q":"A psychiatrist teaching residents states that a close same-sex 'chum' formed around age 10 is necessary for healthy psychological growth, and that lacking one may foreshadow schizophrenia. Whose theory is this?","choices":["Erik Erikson","Michael Rutter","Harry Stack Sullivan","Jean Piaget","Margaret Mahler"],"answer":2,"why":"Harry Stack Sullivan proposed the importance of the buddy or best friend by about age 10 and saw its absence as an early harbinger of schizophrenia (Best friend)."},
  {"id":"c321-ms05","tag":"School age","q":"A 6-year-old boy walks around the house asleep two or three nights a week and remembers nothing in the morning. He is happy, does well at school and has no daytime problems. His parents fear he is emotionally disturbed. What is the best response?","choices":["Advise that it will persist into adulthood","Reassure that this parasomnia is common and does not signal psychopathology","Refer urgently for psychotherapy","Explain that this indicates unresolved oedipal conflict","Start a sleep study to rule out a seizure disorder in all cases"],"answer":1,"why":"Parasomnias such as sleepwalking are common at this age, usually occur in stage 4 sleep, do not indicate underlying psychopathology, and most children outgrow them by adolescence (Dreams and sleep)."},
  {"id":"c321-ms06","tag":"School age","q":"A 3-year-old girl tells her mother she is angry because her mother 'was in the bad dream too and didn't help.' Her 4-year-old cousin, by contrast, says dreams happen only inside your own head. How do these beliefs fit development?","choices":["The 3-year-old has a dream-related anxiety disorder","Both are abnormal for age","The 4-year-old is precocious; this is expected only at age 7","The 3-year-old shows a psychotic belief","Both are typical: many 3-year-olds think dreams are shared, most 4-year-olds know they are personal"],"answer":4,"why":"Many 3-year-olds think two or more people share one dream directly, whereas most 4-year-olds grasp that each dream is personal (Dreams and sleep)."},
  {"id":"c321-ms07","tag":"School age","q":"A 10-year-old boy's teacher notices he ignores her suggestions and the girls in class but quickly changes his behavior when other boys tease him. His female classmate follows the teacher's advice readily. What best explains this pattern?","choices":["Oppositional defiant disorder in the boy","Poor attachment to the mother","Typical sex difference: boys respond to boys, girls to girls and teachers","Sign of a learning disorder","Attention-deficit disorder in the boy"],"answer":2,"why":"Girls usually heed other girls and teachers of both sexes and disregard boys; boys usually heed other boys and disregard girls and teachers (Sex role development)."},
  {"id":"c321-md01","tag":"Divorce and family structure","q":"A 5-year-old boy whose parents separated last month tells his preschool teacher that 'Daddy left because I was bad.' He has otherwise been eating and sleeping normally. Which reaction does this reflect?","choices":["Reunion fantasy typical of adolescents","Typical self-blame of children aged 3 to 6","A sign of conduct disorder","Early sign of psychosis","Normal infant response to changed caregiving"],"answer":1,"why":"Children aged 3 to 6 may not understand divorce, and those who do often assume they are somehow responsible (Children and divorce)."},
  {"id":"c321-md02","tag":"Divorce and family structure","q":"A 4-year-old girl who had been dry at night starts wetting the bed and sucking her thumb after her baby brother is born. She asks her mother to 'send him back.' What is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Normal effect of birth order on personality","Urinary tract infection until proven otherwise","Sexual abuse","Regression related to resentment of a new sibling","Early sign of an anxiety disorder needing medication"],"answer":3,"why":"A firstborn may resent a new sibling who threatens sole claim on parental attention, and the child may regress, for example with bed-wetting or thumb-sucking (Birth order)."},
  {"id":"c321-md03","tag":"Divorce and family structure","q":"A 15-year-old girl whose parents divorced last year says she should have stepped in and stopped their fights. She is angry and critical of both parents and spends most evenings at friends' homes. What is the best interpretation?","choices":["Evidence she caused the divorce","Sign of conduct disorder","Typical adolescent reaction to divorce","Infant-like regression","Romantic step-family pattern"],"answer":2,"why":"Adolescents understand the divorce, may feel they could have prevented it, remain hurt, angry and critical, and are often out of the house more (Children and divorce)."},
  {"id":"c321-md04","tag":"Divorce and family structure","q":"A 9-year-old boy has been hitting classmates since his parents' divorce 6 months ago; before that he had no behavior problems. His mother asks what most helps children adjust. What is the best advice?","choices":["Both parents should make real efforts to spend time with him and avoid arguing with each other","Move him to a new school immediately","Tell him the parents may reunite to reduce his anger","Wait; adaptation is usually complete within weeks","Limit contact with the noncustodial parent so the child can settle"],"answer":0,"why":"Physical aggression is a common distress sign in boys. Children adapt best when both parents genuinely try to spend time with them and when divorced parents stay amicable and avoid arguing; adaptation typically takes several years (Children and divorce)."},
  {"id":"c321-md05","tag":"Divorce and family structure","q":"A newly remarried couple expected to feel like a 'real family' right away. The children's absent father is criticized at home, the stepmother and her stepson fight constantly, and nobody talks openly about the problems. Which step-family type is this?","choices":["Indulgent-neglectful","Matriarchal","Authoritative-reciprocal","Romantic","Neo-traditional"],"answer":3,"why":"Romantic step-families want instant traditional family life, want the other biologic parent gone and criticize him, have common stepparent-stepchild trouble and few frank discussions (Table 32-11)."},
  {"id":"c321-md06","tag":"Divorce and family structure","q":"A mother asks whether her second child's personality will be shaped mainly by being born second. She has read that birth order determines character. What does the evidence show?","choices":["Only firstborns show predictable personality traits","Large multicohort analyses have not supported a birth order-personality link","Second children are reliably more aggressive","Birth order strongly predicts personality","Birth order affects personality only in boys"],"answer":1,"why":"Some researchers proposed a link between birth order and personality, but extensive multicohort analyses have not supported it (Birth order)."},
  {"id":"c321-md07","tag":"Divorce and family structure","q":"A 12-year-old girl whose parents divorced 2 years ago keeps telling friends that her parents will soon get back together. She is openly rude to her mother's new boyfriend. What best explains her behavior?","choices":["Reunion fantasy with hostility toward a new partner","Conduct disorder","Normal acceptance of the divorce","Delusional disorder","Neo-traditional step-family adaptation"],"answer":0,"why":"Some children cling to a wish that the parents will get back together and may be hostile to a parent's actual or possible new partner (Children and divorce)."},
  {"id":"c321-mf01","tag":"Family factors","q":"In a family with marked conflict, two siblings respond differently: a calm, placid 10-year-old girl and a hyperactive 5-year-old boy. Based on Rutter's work, which child is likely more vulnerable?","choices":["Neither, since temperament has no role","Both equally, since family instability affects all children the same","The boy, because he is younger, male and hyperactive","The girl, because she is older","The girl, because girls are more affected than boys"],"answer":2,"why":"Rutter proposed that boys suffer more than girls, younger children are more vulnerable than older ones, and placid children are abused less often than hyperactive ones (Family stability)."},
  {"id":"c321-mf02","tag":"Family factors","q":"Parents describe their home as one with strict, inflexible rules and no room for discussion. Their 11-year-old son is unhappy, withdrawn and has low self-esteem. Which parenting style is most consistent with this picture?","choices":["Authoritative-reciprocal","Authoritarian","Indulgent-neglectful","Matriarchal","Indulgent-permissive"],"answer":1,"why":"Authoritarian parenting, built on rigid rules, is linked to low self-esteem, unhappiness and withdrawal from others (Parenting styles)."},
  {"id":"c321-mf03","tag":"Family factors","q":"A 7-year-old girl has poor impulse control and is often aggressive. Her parents set almost no limits but occasionally explode with harsh punishment without warning. Which parenting style is described?","choices":["Neo-traditional","Indulgent-neglectful","Indulgent-permissive","Authoritarian","Authoritative-reciprocal"],"answer":2,"why":"Indulgent-permissive parenting pairs few or no limits with harshness that comes without warning; it is linked to weak self-reliance, aggression and poor impulse control (Parenting styles)."},
  {"id":"c321-mf04","tag":"Family factors","q":"A family sets firm rules but involves their 13-year-old in decisions, in a warm and affectionate home. Which outcome is this style believed most likely to produce?","choices":["Low self-esteem","Self-reliance, self-esteem and social responsibility","Social withdrawal","Increased aggression","Poor impulse control"],"answer":1,"why":"Authoritative-reciprocal parenting (firm rules, decisions made together, a warm and loving home) is thought most likely to yield self-reliance, self-esteem and social responsibility (Parenting styles)."},
  {"id":"c321-mf05","tag":"Family factors","q":"A 20-month-old boy says no words and does not seem to follow simple spoken requests, though he walks and plays well. His parents ask whether to wait. What is the best next step?","choices":["Arrange a developmental language assessment","Reassure; words are not expected until age 3","Start treatment for bipolar disorder","Diagnose schizophrenia","Wait until he enters elementary school"],"answer":0,"why":"Evaluation may be needed for a child without words by 18 months or without phrases by 2½ to 3 years, all the more if ordinary spoken cues seem not to be understood (Development and expression of psychopathology)."},
  {"id":"c321-mf06","tag":"Family factors","q":"A 7-year-old boy did well in preschool but in first grade cannot stay on task during lessons and seatwork. His parents ask why problems appeared only now. What is the best explanation?","choices":["Bipolar disorder often presents first in early school years","This is a reaction to daycare before age 5","Developmental language disorders are usually first diagnosed in grade school","Schizophrenia commonly begins at this age","Attention-deficit disorders may only become apparent when school demands sustained attention"],"answer":4,"why":"Attention-deficit disorders may first show at school, where long periods of focus are required; schizophrenia and bipolar disorder are rare at this age and language disorders are often diagnosed in the preschool years (Development and expression of psychopathology)."},
  {"id":"c321-mf07","tag":"Family factors","q":"A 4½-year-old girl has attended full-time child care for more than 30 hours a week since infancy. Her preschool teacher finds her more demanding and noncompliant than peers, but her early reading and math are ahead. Which statement best fits the NICHD findings?","choices":["Daycare reliably lowers cognitive skills","These findings apply regardless of daycare and home quality","She has a conduct disorder caused by daycare","This pattern was reported, and the behavior stayed within the normal range","She will have no differences in social skills by third grade"],"answer":3,"why":"NICHD reported that 4½-year-olds with more than 30 hours a week of child care were more demanding, aggressive and noncompliant but had higher cognitive skills, especially math and reading; the researchers noted the behavior was within the normal range, and quality of center and home must be considered (Daycare centers)."},
  {"id":"c321-an01","tag":"Normal adolescence","q":"A 15-year-old girl tells her pediatrician she sometimes feels sad and worried. She has good grades, close friends, and says she is mostly happy with her life. Her mother asks whether this means she has a psychiatric disorder. Which statement best fits this picture?","choices":["This pattern shows a failure of ego identity","This pattern predicts identity diffusion in late adolescence","This pattern indicates over-identification with an idol","This pattern meets the threshold for an affective disorder","Occasional distress like this is common in teens who function well"],"answer":4,"why":"Up to 60 percent of adolescents report occasional distress or a symptom yet function well at school and with peers and feel satisfied with life (What Is Normal Adolescence?)."},
  {"id":"c321-an02","tag":"Normal adolescence","q":"A child psychiatrist is asked by parents of a 12-year-old who had marked behavioral and emotional problems through primary school whether puberty will \"reset\" things. Which response best fits current understanding?","choices":["Puberty usually erases earlier problems","Earlier problems have no bearing on teen outcomes","Risk is raised only once he reaches late adolescence","Risk is raised only for boys","Teen adjustment continues from earlier function, so his risk is higher"],"answer":4,"why":"Adolescent adjustment is continuous with earlier function, so children who were psychologically troubled face greater odds of psychiatric illness as teens (What Is Normal Adolescence?)."},
  {"id":"c321-an03","tag":"Normal adolescence","q":"A 16-year-old boy has no friends, says classmates reject him, and has quit sports and clubs. He spends all his free time on one rock singer, and nothing else interests him. Which is the best next step?","choices":["Refer for psychiatric assessment and intervention","Reassure the parents that fandom is normal","Limit his music and recheck in a year","Wait to see whether it passes by late adolescence","Encourage him to find a second idol"],"answer":0,"why":"Isolated teens who feel rejected and are absorbed by one idol, having dropped other pursuits, face higher risk of serious emotional trouble and need psychiatric intervention (What Is Normal Adolescence?)."},
  {"id":"c321-an04","tag":"Normal adolescence","q":"A 15-year-old girl dyes her hair, follows a famous political activist closely, and tries out several new styles and clubs over a year. She remains in a stable friend group and keeps her grades. By Erikson's model, what is she most likely going through?","choices":["Alienation from family","Identity diffusion","A normative identity crisis","Role confusion","Social deprivation"],"answer":2,"why":"Erikson saw the identity crisis as a normal part of adolescence in which teens try alternative behaviors and styles and mold them into a stable identity (What Is Normal Adolescence?)."},
  {"id":"c321-an05","tag":"Normal adolescence","q":"A 19-year-old man drifts between groups and plans, cannot say what he values, and feels he has no clear or confident sense of who he is. Earlier attempts to try out roles never came together. In Erikson's terms, what does this describe?","choices":["Ego identity","Identity diffusion","Moratorium","Identity crisis","Omnipotence"],"answer":1,"why":"When the teen fails to mold experiences into a stable identity, Erikson described identity diffusion, or role confusion: a lack of a cohesive, confident identity (What Is Normal Adolescence?)."},
  {"id":"c321-an06","tag":"Normal adolescence","q":"A resident teaching medical students explains why a 12-year-old girl may read subtle social cues and plan ahead better than she did at 9. Which brain change in adolescence is most relevant?","choices":["Total cortical gray matter peaks around this age in girls","Gray matter peaks at about 13 years in girls","Neural tube closure is completed in adolescence","White matter stops growing in late childhood","White matter volume falls sharply in early adolescence"],"answer":0,"why":"Total cortical gray matter peaks at about age 11 in girls (13 in boys), enhancing understanding of subtle social situations, impulse control and planning; white matter keeps increasing (Adolescence introduction)."},
  {"id":"c321-an07","tag":"Normal adolescence","q":"A 17-year-old boy with depression and substance use has frequent fights at home and says he does not feel part of his family. His parents ask if these family problems are unusual. Which statement fits best?","choices":["His alienation proves his identity crisis is complete","Family conflict is unrelated to his psychiatric disorder","Teens with psychiatric disorders have more family conflict and alienation","Most teens with good adjustment feel alienated from family","Family alienation is the expected norm for all teens"],"answer":2,"why":"Teens with psychiatric disorders tend to have more serious family conflict and to feel alienated, whereas most teens sustain harmonious family relationships (What Is Normal Adolescence?)."},
  {"id":"c321-an08","tag":"Normal adolescence","q":"A group of parents asks a psychiatrist what proportion of teenagers develop impairing psychiatric disorders such as conduct problems, substance abuse or affective disorders. Which figure fits best?","choices":["About 45 percent","About 20 percent","About 75 percent","About 5 percent","About 60 percent"],"answer":1,"why":"Impairing psychiatric disorders emerge in about 20 percent of adolescents; up to 75 percent adapt successfully, and up to 60 percent report occasional symptoms while functioning well (What Is Normal Adolescence?)."},
  {"id":"c321-as01","tag":"Adolescent stages","q":"A 13-year-old boy, previously cooperative, now calls school rules stupid, cuts classes, drops old friends for daring peers and tries alcohol. His teachers often compare him with his compliant older brother. What best explains his change?","choices":["Late-adolescent career exploration","Normal toddler-type negativism","Identity diffusion typical of late adolescence","Early-adolescent push for autonomy and need to differ from a sibling","Middle-adolescent sense of omnipotence about driving"],"answer":3,"why":"Early adolescence (12 to 14) brings challenges to authority and rules and first substance experiments; in the source case, the boy rebelled to set himself apart from his brother (Early Adolescence)."},
  {"id":"c321-as02","tag":"Adolescent stages","q":"A 13-year-old boy is caught with a small amount of marijuana at school. He argues the suspension is unfair because his grades are good and says everyone favors his older brother. The school says it will report to police unless counseling starts. Which plan best matches the approach that helped in a similar case?","choices":["Medication alone for his anger","Waiting until he turns 15 to begin therapy","Individual therapy, a teen substance-use group, and parent therapy on unified parenting","Immediate transfer to another school without treatment","Parent therapy only, since he is too young for groups"],"answer":2,"why":"In the early-adolescence case, the teen entered psychotherapy and a weekly substance-use group, and his parents sought therapy to parent in a more unified way; over 1 and a half years his reasoning matured (Early Adolescence)."},
  {"id":"c321-as03","tag":"Adolescent stages","q":"A 12-year-old girl becomes very modest, refuses to change in front of her sister, and is embarrassed by her developing body. Her parents worry. What is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Normal new awareness of sexuality in early adolescence","Sign of a late-adolescent identity crisis","Middle-adolescent sexual pressure","Early sign of identity diffusion","Social isolation needing urgent treatment"],"answer":0,"why":"In early adolescence, new sexual awareness may appear as increased modesty and embarrassment about current physical development (Early Adolescence)."},
  {"id":"c321-as04","tag":"Adolescent stages","q":"A mother notes that her 13-year-old son has just begun a growth spurt, while his twin sister shot up a year or two earlier. What does this most likely reflect?","choices":["Precocious puberty in the girl","Late-adolescent growth pattern","Growth delay in the boy needing workup","Normal sex difference in early-adolescent growth timing","Effect of peer affiliation on growth"],"answer":3,"why":"Growth spurts often begin in early adolescence for boys, whereas girls may already have been growing quickly for 1 to 2 years (Early Adolescence)."},
  {"id":"c321-as05","tag":"Adolescent stages","q":"A 14-year-old girl suddenly rejects her family, all her old friends and her former lifestyle, and feels deeply cut off from everyone. Her school counselor asks how common this is. Which statement fits best?","choices":["Most early teens keep positive ties; this marks turmoil in a minority","This happens only in late adolescence","This is required for a healthy identity","This pattern is expected after a growth spurt","Most early teens show this degree of rejection"],"answer":0,"why":"Most early adolescents keep positive connections to family, friends and values; for some, the phase brings overwhelming turmoil with dramatic rejection and powerful alienation (Early Adolescence)."},
  {"id":"c321-as06","tag":"Adolescent stages","q":"A 15-year-old boy and his friends ride bikes recklessly on busy roads, insisting nothing bad will happen to them. His parents' rules lead to frequent arguments. Which feature of his developmental stage best explains this?","choices":["Middle-adolescent underestimation of risk and sense of omnipotence","Early-adolescent modesty","Normative identity diffusion","Peak white matter loss","Late-adolescent comfort with current choices"],"answer":0,"why":"In middle adolescence (about 14 to 16), underestimating risk and a sense of omnipotence, mixed with the drive for autonomy, often cause conflict with parents (Middle Adolescence)."},
  {"id":"c321-as07","tag":"Adolescent stages","q":"A 16-year-old girl with good self-esteem is pressured by her boyfriend to have sex. She tells him calmly she is not ready, accepting that he may leave her. Which developmental factor most likely helped her?","choices":["Parental refusal to discuss sex","Identity diffusion","Early-adolescent rejection of peers","Self-esteem shaping her approach to risk","A peak in white matter at age 16"],"answer":3,"why":"In middle adolescence, sexual behavior intensifies and self-esteem is pivotal for both good and harmful risk-taking; in the source case, the confident teen held her limit (Middle Adolescence)."},
  {"id":"c321-as08","tag":"Adolescent stages","q":"A 15-year-old boy changes his clothes, music and role models to match his new friend group, which now guides most of his choices. He still eats dinner with his family and talks with his parents. How is this best described?","choices":["Typical middle-adolescent peer influence with family ties kept","Pathological alienation from family","Identity diffusion","Early-adolescent rejection of family values","Late-adolescent subculture belonging"],"answer":0,"why":"In middle adolescence the peer group strongly shapes what teens do, wear and listen to, and whom they admire, and most teens define themselves as unique while staying connected to family (Middle Adolescence)."},
  {"id":"c321-as09","tag":"Adolescent stages","q":"An 18-year-old college freshman hated his premed science courses, then took varied classes and loves drafting. He is unsure whether he truly likes it or just admires his architect roommate. Which approach best supports his development?","choices":["Advise him to avoid classes his friends take","Tell him to choose a major this week","Allow more time to sample courses before deciding","Refer for treatment of identity diffusion","Insist he commit to medicine as planned"],"answer":2,"why":"Late adolescence (17 to 19) is a time of continued exploration that leads to a clearer definition of self; in the source case, taking another year to explore led to a choice he truly enjoyed (Late Adolescence)."},
  {"id":"c321-as10","tag":"Adolescent stages","q":"A 19-year-old woman is happy with her current hobbies, friends and art group, but says she expects her sense of who she is to keep changing in her twenties. How is this best viewed?","choices":["Sign of role confusion","Delayed early-adolescent development","Middle-adolescent omnipotence","Healthy late-adolescent adjustment","Sign of identity diffusion"],"answer":3,"why":"Well-adjusted older teens can feel at ease with present choices while knowing they will keep refining their identities in young adulthood (Late Adolescence)."},
  {"id":"c321-as11","tag":"Adolescent stages","q":"A 16-year-old girl tells her doctor she is not yet ready for sex, though many classmates are sexually active. She plans carefully and dislikes impulsive choices. Her parents have brushed off the topic. Which phase best fits her age and situation?","choices":["Early adolescence","Young adulthood","School age","Late adolescence","Middle adolescence"],"answer":4,"why":"Middle adolescence runs roughly 14 to 16; sexual behavior intensifies and romance becomes more complicated (Middle Adolescence)."},
  {"id":"c321-ac01","tag":"Adolescent maturation","q":"A mother brings her 11-year-old daughter and 11-year-old son (twins). The daughter is now much taller than her brother and his classmates, and the mother worries the boy is not growing. He is otherwise healthy. What is the best explanation?","choices":["The boy likely has a growth hormone deficiency","The girl has precocious puberty needing workup","Boys' growth spurt typically comes later, around 13 years","Twins of opposite sex usually grow at identical rates","Boys normally reach their adult height before girls"],"answer":2,"why":"Girls begin the growth spurt at about 10 and often tower over boys at 11 or 12; boys on average spurt at about 13 (s321-adcomp)."},
  {"id":"c321-ac02","tag":"Adolescent maturation","q":"A 13-year-old girl has breasts and areolae that are larger, with no separation of their contours, and darker, coarser pubic hair spread over the pubic area. Hair does not reach the thighs. What is her sexual maturity rating?","choices":["SMR 1","SMR 5","SMR 4","SMR 3","SMR 2"],"answer":3,"why":"Enlarged breast and areola without separate contours plus darker, coarser hair over the pubic area is SMR 3 (Table 32-12)."},
  {"id":"c321-ac03","tag":"Adolescent maturation","q":"A 12-year-old boy has enlarged testes and scrotum with reddened scrotal skin; penile size is unchanged and there are a few long hairs at the base of the penis. What is his sexual maturity rating?","choices":["SMR 4","SMR 2","SMR 1","SMR 5","SMR 3"],"answer":1,"why":"Testicular and scrotal enlargement with reddened skin, unchanged penis and sparse hair at the penile base define SMR 2 (Table 32-12)."},
  {"id":"c321-ac04","tag":"Adolescent maturation","q":"A 16-year-old girl has adult-type breasts whose areola has recessed to the breast contour, and dense pubic hair that now extends to the thighs. Which statement about her development is correct?","choices":["She is at SMR 1 and puberty has not yet started","She is at SMR 3 and has not yet reached menarche","She is at SMR 2 and ovulation will begin within a year","She is at SMR 5, the adult stage, when monthly ovulation is expected","She is at SMR 4 and menarche is still years away"],"answer":3,"why":"Recessed areola and hair to the thighs mark SMR 5 (adult); monthly ovulation occurs at SMR 4 to 5 (s321-adcomp, Table 32-12)."},
  {"id":"c321-ac05","tag":"Adolescent maturation","q":"A 15-year-old who excels in algebra and debate rides his bike at night without lights on a highway, saying nothing bad will happen to him. He can explain the statistics of crashes. Which concept best explains this?","choices":["Persistent preoperational egocentrism","Failure to reach concrete operations","An omnipotent belief system coexisting with abstract thought","Gilligan's ethic of caring","Kohlberg's conventional role-conformity"],"answer":2,"why":"Teens often hold an omnipotent belief system that makes them feel immune from danger despite logical risks; magical thinking coexists with formal operations (s321-adcomp)."},
  {"id":"c321-ac06","tag":"Adolescent maturation","q":"A 17-year-old explains that she would break a school rule to help a classmate in danger, because ethical principles matter more than the rule itself. Which Kohlberg level does this reflect?","choices":["Self-accepted moral principles","Conventional role-conformity","Rule acceptance without exceptions","Preconventional morality","Punishment and obedience"],"answer":0,"why":"At Kohlberg's highest level, persons follow rules out of ethical principle and make exceptions in certain circumstances (s321-adcomp)."},
  {"id":"c321-ac07","tag":"Adolescent maturation","q":"A 9-year-old insists a board game rule cannot be bent even when all players agree to change it. He accepts the rules fully. In Piaget's view of moral development, which period does this fit?","choices":["Preschool, simply following parents' rules","Late adolescence, weighing society against the individual","Adolescence, judging rules by the good of society","Middle years, accepting rules but allowing no exceptions","Infancy, before rules are understood"],"answer":3,"why":"Piaget described middle-years children as accepting rules but unable to allow exceptions (s321-adcomp)."},
  {"id":"c321-ac08","tag":"Adolescent maturation","q":"A 14-year-old girl with few friends at home starts spending all her time with an older group known for skipping school, saying 'they actually like me.' Her parents are alarmed. What best explains her choice?","choices":["She is at Kohlberg's self-accepted principles level","Joining any peer group is always a sign of psychopathology","Academic success is the strongest correlate of teen self-esteem","Teens rarely care about peer acceptance","Teen self-esteem rests heavily on acceptance and positive feedback from a peer group"],"answer":4,"why":"Teen self-esteem is mediated largely by peer and family feedback, and teens often seek a group that accepts them regardless of its negative behaviors (s321-adcomp)."},
  {"id":"c321-ax01","tag":"Adolescent sexuality","q":"A 14-year-old girl seen for abdominal pain discloses that she first had intercourse at 13 with an older boyfriend. She becomes tearful and vague when asked about it. What is the most important next step for the clinician?","choices":["Focus only on contraception counseling","Defer the topic until she turns 16","Reassure her that first sex at 13 is typical","Gently ask whether the experience was voluntary and assess her safety","Inform her that most girls her age are sexually active"],"answer":3,"why":"Close to 4 in 10 girls with first intercourse at 13 or 14 report it was unwanted or not voluntary; earlier age raises this likelihood (s321-adsex)."},
  {"id":"c321-ax02","tag":"Adolescent sexuality","q":"A 16-year-old boy with marked impulsivity is seen in clinic. Based on the association between personality and sexual behavior, which history is he most likely to have?","choices":["Abstinence for religious reasons","Fewer sexual partners than average","Later first intercourse than his peers","Intercourse without contraception and a past STD such as chlamydia","Consistent condom use"],"answer":3,"why":"Higher impulsivity is linked to younger first sex, more partners, no contraception and a history of an STD (s321-adsex)."},
  {"id":"c321-ax03","tag":"Adolescent sexuality","q":"A 15-year-old girl says she has never had intercourse and has no plans to. When asked why, which reason is she most likely to give, based on survey data?","choices":["Her parents would punish her","Sex would go against her religious or moral values","She is not interested in relationships","She has no access to contraceptives","Her friends have advised against it"],"answer":1,"why":"The primary reason never-active girls give is that sex would go against religious or moral values (s321-adsex)."},
  {"id":"c321-ax04","tag":"Adolescent sexuality","q":"A 17-year-old girl reports always using condoms with her partner and asks if she still needs STD screening. Which fact is most relevant to your answer?","choices":["Condoms are rarely used by teenagers","STDs are rare among teens who use any birth control","Birth control pills protect against STDs","About one in four sexually active teens acquires an STD each year despite condom use","Only teens over 20 contract HIV"],"answer":3,"why":"Despite condoms, about 1 in 4 sexually active teens gets an STD yearly, and about half of new HIV infections occur under 25 (s321-adsex)."},
  {"id":"c321-ax05","tag":"Adolescent sexuality","q":"A pregnant 16-year-old attends her first prenatal visit at 30 weeks. She has gained little weight, takes no vitamins, and smokes. Which outcome is her infant at highest risk for?","choices":["Better school performance","Low birth weight with respiratory distress syndrome","Postmaturity","High birth weight","Lower risk of abuse"],"answer":1,"why":"Poor maternal weight gain raises risk of prematurity and low birth weight, with immature organs leading to brain bleeding, respiratory distress syndrome and intestinal problems (s321-adsex)."},
  {"id":"c321-ax06","tag":"Adolescent sexuality","q":"A 16-year-old in the United States seeks an abortion; her parents refuse consent. Based on the law as this chapter describes it (before the 2022 Dobbs decision), what generally applied?","choices":["Federal law requires both parents to sign","Most states do not allow parents to veto the teen's decision","Parents in all states may veto the decision","Most adults believe teens need no parental permission","Teens may never seek abortion without parental involvement"],"answer":1,"why":"Most adults favor parental permission, but when parents refused, most states as the chapter describes them barred a parental veto; the laws weighed privacy against the parent's need to know (s321-adsex). Since Dobbs (2022), state rules differ widely; check current local law (see the update box)."},
  {"id":"c321-ar01","tag":"Risk-taking","q":"A 15-year-old boy tries out for a new sport and joins an unfamiliar club, which makes him anxious. His mother asks if this risk-taking is a concern. What is the best response?","choices":["It requires referral for impulsivity","It is a warning sign of conduct problems","It reflects reasonable risk-taking that builds confidence and is necessary in adolescence","It is a form of self-injurious behavior","It predicts later reckless driving"],"answer":2,"why":"Reasonable risk-taking is necessary in adolescence and builds confidence in new relationships, sports and social situations (s321-adrisk)."},
  {"id":"c321-ar02","tag":"Risk-taking","q":"A 17-year-old is brought in after his third speeding citation. Which statement best describes his behavior?","choices":["A behavior seen only in early adolescence","A sign of formal operational thinking","A form of healthy peer socialization","A high-risk behavior with potentially severe negative consequences","A normal, necessary form of adolescent risk"],"answer":3,"why":"Reckless driving is listed among high-risk behaviors linked to severe negative consequences (s321-adrisk)."},
  {"id":"c321-au01","tag":"Adolescent substance use","q":"A 17-year-old boy is brought in after an overdose of alcohol and pills. He has been drinking heavily for 2 years. His mother asks which problems are known to go along with alcohol dependence in teens. Which condition is listed in the chapter as associated with adolescent alcohol dependence?","choices":["Reactive attachment disorder","Encopresis","Selective mutism","Oppositional defiant disorder","Pica"],"answer":3,"why":"Alcohol and other drug dependence are tied to depression, anxiety, ODD, antisocial personality disorder and more suicide (s321-addrug)."},
  {"id":"c321-au02","tag":"Adolescent substance use","q":"A 13-year-old girl admits to smoking cigarettes with friends. Her father asks what other risks the doctor should ask about. Which behavior is more likely in teen smokers?","choices":["Carrying weapons","Less fighting with peers","Lower rates of depression","Lower rates of suicide attempts","Fewer high-risk sexual behaviors"],"answer":0,"why":"Teen cigarette smokers are more likely to fight, carry weapons, attempt suicide, have depression and take sexual risks (s321-addrug)."},
  {"id":"c321-au03","tag":"Adolescent substance use","q":"A school nurse surveys tobacco use in a middle school in the United States. Which tobacco product would she expect to be most common among the students?","choices":["Smokeless tobacco","Hookah","Cigars","Cigarettes","Pipes"],"answer":3,"why":"In middle school, cigarettes lead, then cigars, then smokeless tobacco, then pipes (s321-addrug)."},
  {"id":"c321-au04","tag":"Adolescent substance use","q":"A 16-year-old boy says he uses marijuana because it is easier to get than beer or cigarettes. Over the next year he stops attending school and is arrested for shoplifting. Which further outcome does the chapter link to teen marijuana dependence?","choices":["Anorexia nervosa","Tic disorder","Enuresis","Depression","Mania"],"answer":3,"why":"Marijuana-dependent teens often slide into skipping school, crime and depression (s321-addrug)."},
  {"id":"c321-au05","tag":"Adolescent substance use","q":"An 18-year-old in a rehabilitation program reports using heroin. Which route of use is most common among adolescents who use heroin?","choices":["Smoking","Intravenous injection","Intranasal snorting","Subcutaneous injection","Oral ingestion"],"answer":2,"why":"Snorting (the nasal route) is the most common method of heroin use in teens (s321-addrug)."},
  {"id":"c321-av01","tag":"Youth violence","q":"A 15-year-old boy is referred after repeated assaults on classmates. A resident reviews risk factors for violence in adolescent boys. Which factor is most strongly associated with violence in this group?","choices":["Belonging to a particular racial group","Lower educational level","Growing up without a father or father surrogate","Being the youngest child","Low family income"],"answer":2,"why":"Absence of a father or father surrogate is the factor most strongly tied to violence in teen boys; apart from this, race, SES and education show no effect (s321-adviol)."},
  {"id":"c321-av02","tag":"Youth violence","q":"A 12-year-old girl cries before school each day. Classmates have started ignoring her and telling others not to sit with her at lunch. No one has hit or insulted her. Which form of bullying is this?","choices":["Cyberbullying","Social bullying","Verbal bullying","Dating violence","Physical bullying"],"answer":1,"why":"Social bullying shames or isolates the target by having peers reject or shut them out (s321-adviol)."},
  {"id":"c321-av03","tag":"Youth violence","q":"A 14-year-old boy is harassed through group chats and posts by classmates. His parents ask which problems this puts him at risk for. According to the chapter's cited study, what does cyberbullying involvement specifically help predict?","choices":["Improved school grades","Lower rates of traditional bullying","Eating disorders","Psychotic symptoms","Depressive symptoms and suicidal ideation"],"answer":4,"why":"In a study of 399 teens, cyberbullying as victim or bully predicted depressive symptoms and suicidal ideation, more reliably than traditional bullying did (s321-adviol)."},
  {"id":"c321-av04","tag":"Youth violence","q":"A 15-year-old girl in a small rural town is found to belong to a local gang. Her mother fears she will be in it for life. Which statement about youth gang membership is correct?","choices":["Girls join gangs more often than boys","Most members stay for at least 5 years","Many teens leave the gang within a year","Most members are older than 25","Female members commit more violent crimes than males"],"answer":2,"why":"For many teens gang membership is a brief phase; one-half to two-thirds leave by 1 year. Most members are 12 to 24, boys join more often, and female members commit fewer violent crimes (s321-adviol)."},
  {"id":"c321-av05","tag":"Youth violence","q":"A 16-year-old boy with depression and suicidal thoughts is seen in clinic. His father keeps several handguns at home. Which statement about firearms and teens is supported by the chapter?","choices":["Firearm deaths in children are rare, under one a month","Teens rarely can obtain a gun within a day","Most school shooters bought guns at stores","Handguns are used in at least 60 percent of teen suicide deaths","Guns in homes with children are almost always locked"],"answer":3,"why":"At least 60 percent of teen suicide deaths involve a handgun; two-thirds of students say they could get a gun within 24 hours, and two of three school shooters took guns from home or a relative (s321-adviol)."},
  {"id":"c321-av06","tag":"Youth violence","q":"A school counselor reviews a 14-year-old boy's file. He has drawn violent pictures, feels picked on and has a history of bullying others. This week he made a detailed threat to kill a classmate. Which finding is an imminent, not just early, warning sign?","choices":["Feeling picked on","A history of bullying","Violent drawings","A detailed threat of lethal violence","Social withdrawal"],"answer":3,"why":"Detailed threats of lethal violence are an imminent warning sign; the others are early signs in Table 32-13 (s321-adviol)."},
  {"id":"c321-av07","tag":"Youth violence","q":"A 9-year-old boy has seen repeated violence between adults at home. His teacher reports he is now hitting other children. Which outcome does the chapter link to witnessing violence?","choices":["Protection against later violence","No measurable effect","Greater aggression and later involvement in violence as victim or victimizer","Greater fearfulness and avoidance of conflict only","Higher academic achievement"],"answer":2,"why":"Children who witness violence become more aggressive and are more likely to be drawn into violence, as victimizer or victim (s321-adviol)."},
  {"id":"c321-ao01","tag":"Sexual offending and exploitation","q":"A 16-year-old boy is assessed after a sexual assault on an adult woman he did not know, during a burglary. He has prior arrests for theft and fights. Which feature best fits his offender subtype?","choices":["More likely to use weapons and injure victims","Victims are mostly related young boys","Low aggression with prominent depression","Offenses rely on guile rather than force","Offenses usually occur in the family home"],"answer":0,"why":"Youths who offend against peers or adults mostly target female strangers, often during other crimes, in public, with more violence and weapon use (Table 32-14)."},
  {"id":"c321-ao02","tag":"Sexual offending and exploitation","q":"A 15-year-old boy is referred after his 7-year-old cousin disclosed abuse. He had bribed the child and threatened to stop playing with him. He is socially awkward with low self-esteem. Which statement best fits his offender subtype?","choices":["Often shows depression and poor social competence","Mostly assaults female strangers","Has a long history of nonsexual crime","Commonly uses weapons","Usually offends in public places"],"answer":0,"why":"Youths who offend against children mostly target related boys using opportunity and guile, and often have low self-esteem, poor social competence and depression (Table 32-14)."},
  {"id":"c321-ao03","tag":"Sexual offending and exploitation","q":"A resident prepares an evaluation of a 15-year-old girl who sexually abused a younger child. Compared with male adolescent offenders, which finding is she more likely to show?","choices":["Paraphilia","Gang affiliation","Antisocial behavior","Weapon use","Mood disorder and self-mutilation"],"answer":4,"why":"Female adolescent offenders are more likely to have mood disorders and self-mutilation; males more often have paraphilias and antisocial behavior (s321-adsexo)."},
  {"id":"c321-ao04","tag":"Sexual offending and exploitation","q":"A 14-year-old girl is brought to the emergency room by police after being found in a parking lot with an older man. She ran away from home months ago, uses drugs and reports childhood abuse. Which risk should the team keep most in mind, per the chapter?","choices":["Iron overload","Lead poisoning","Hypothyroidism","HIV infection","Celiac disease"],"answer":3,"why":"Teen prostitutes are at high risk for AIDS; up to 70 percent have HIV in some studies. Runaway status, substance use and past abuse fit the chapter's profile (s321-adsexo)."},
  {"id":"c321-ao05","tag":"Sexual offending and exploitation","q":"A 15-year-old boy in custody for a sexual offense against a peer is evaluated. Which psychiatric diagnosis is listed among the most common in adolescent sexual offenders?","choices":["Conduct disorder","Anorexia nervosa","Selective mutism","Schizophrenia","Delirium"],"answer":0,"why":"The usual diagnoses are conduct disorder, ADHD, substance abuse disorder, mood disorders, adjustment disorder and specific phobia (s321-adsexo)."},
  {"id":"c321-ab01","tag":"Body modification","q":"A 16-year-old girl comes for a sports physical. She has a new navel piercing and a small tattoo on her ankle. She is well and has no complaints. Based on the survey data on teen body modification, which further history is most important to take?","choices":["Number of hours spent on homework","History of childhood ear infections","Family history of skin cancer","Preferred sports position","Tobacco, alcohol, cannabis and other drug use"],"answer":4,"why":"Teens who report a tattoo or a body piercing are more likely to report gateway drugs (cigarettes, alcohol, marijuana) and hard drugs (cocaine, ecstasy, crystal methamphetamine) (section 32.1, tattoos and body piercing)."},
  {"id":"c321-ab02","tag":"Body modification","q":"A school health nurse reviews data from a survey of more than 500 adolescents about body modification. She wants to know which was more common over the lifetime. Which finding matches the survey?","choices":["Non-earlobe piercings 26.9 percent, tattoos 13.2 percent","Non-earlobe piercings 13.2 percent, tattoos 2 percent","Tattoos 26.9 percent, non-earlobe piercings 13.2 percent","Tattoos 10 percent, non-earlobe piercings 10 percent","Tattoos and non-earlobe piercings both about 13 percent"],"answer":0,"why":"In the survey, 26.9 percent reported at least one piercing outside the earlobe and 13.2 percent at least one tattoo, so piercing was about twice as common (section 32.1, tattoos and body piercing)."},
  {"id":"c321-ab03","tag":"Sexual offending and exploitation","q":"A 17-year-old girl is brought to the emergency room with injuries from a sexual assault. She says she came to the United States after being promised a job, but the people who brought her make her have sex with men for money and keep almost all of it. Which is the most likely situation?","choices":["Voluntary part-time sex work","Sex trafficking","Gang initiation","Runaway behavior without exploitation","Consensual dating violence"],"answer":1,"why":"Victims are lured by promises of work or a better life, then made to sell sex once in the United States; they earn little while traffickers make thousands of dollars, and many are raped and abused (section 32.1, prostitution and trafficking)."}
]);
